---
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import MissFigure from '../../components/application-caching/misses-pilot/MissFigure.astro';
const { hrefFor } = Astro.props;
const wrapper = `type Card string // a rendered product card

// Group shares one source load per key among the callers waiting for it.
type Group struct {
	sf    singleflight.Group
	Load  func(ctx context.Context, key string) (Card, error) // recheck cache, read source, fill
	Stale func(key string) (Card, bool)                      // a copy freshness still permits
}

func (g *Group) Get(ctx context.Context, key string) (Card, error) {
	ch := g.sf.DoChan(key, func() (any, error) {
		// Detach from the first caller, then give the shared load its own budget.
		lctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.WithoutCancel(ctx), 120*time.Millisecond)
		defer cancel()
		return g.Load(lctx, key)
	})
	select {
	case r := <-ch:
		if r.Err != nil {
			return "", r.Err
		}
		return r.Val.(Card), nil
	case <-ctx.Done(): // this caller's deadline: leave without starting another read
		if c, ok := g.Stale(key); ok {
			return c, nil
		}
		return "", ctx.Err()
	}
}`;
const stampedeActivity = `select wait_event, count(*), min(clock_timestamp() - query_start) as newest,
       max(clock_timestamp() - query_start) as oldest, left(query, 40) as query
from pg_stat_activity
where backend_type = 'client backend' and state = 'active' and pid <> pg_backend_pid()
group by 1, 5 order by 2 desc;

 wait_event | count |     newest      |     oldest      |                  query
------------+-------+-----------------+-----------------+------------------------------------------
 PgSleep    |   233 | 00:00:00.003088 | 00:00:00.584587 | select name || ':' || price_pence /*card`;
const leaseTrace = `   0 ms  A  SET lease:list:… tokA NX PX 200   -> OK
  11 ms  B  SET lease:list:… tokB NX PX 200   -> (nil)   serves the older list
 212 ms  B  SET lease:list:… tokB NX PX 200   -> OK
 364 ms  B  owner-checked publish, release    -> 1, 1
 902 ms  A  owner-checked publish, release    -> 0, 0    cache keeps B’s list`;
const publishCheck = `-- KEYS[1] lease, KEYS[2] list; ARGV[1] token, ARGV[2] list, ARGV[3] ms to the hard limit
if redis.call('GET', KEYS[1]) == ARGV[1] then
  redis.call('SET', KEYS[2], ARGV[2], 'PX', ARGV[3])
  return 1
end
return 0`;
---
<p>A cache miss sends a request back to the work the cache was meant to save. At the shop’s peak, 20,000 product-card requests a second reach 20 <code>Server</code> instances. About 94% of them find a usable card in Redis, and the other 1,200 a second read PostgreSQL, assemble the card and store it again. A load test found that the database could sustain about 2,500 card fills a second before checkout missed its latency objective, so the card cache is allowed 2,000 fills a second.</p>
<p>That margin is comfortable on an average second. Misses do damage when they arrive together: when a popular entry expires, when Redis restarts empty, or when it becomes unreachable. The source then receives a burst that the average hit ratio never shows, and slowing under it makes the burst last longer.</p>
<p>Handling misses means deciding what each caller does when its answer is missing. It can start source work or wait for someone else’s, take an older answer that is still permitted or a reduced one, or be refused quickly. Request coalescing, deadlines, early refresh and admission control make those decisions, and they must keep protecting the source when the cache fails.</p>
<p>Two cached answers carry the examples. Product P7’s card, the blue mug, is requested about 250 times a second and takes 20 ms to fill. Shop north’s list of products under GBP 21.00 is linked from the home page. It is requested about 400 times a second and takes about 150 ms of database time to compute.</p>

<section aria-labelledby="one-expiry">
<h2 id="one-expiry">One expiry can start many reads</h2>
<p>The list has just expired, and the first request misses and starts computing it. The next arrives 2.5 ms later and finds the entry still missing, because nothing in the cache records that the first has begun, so it starts a second computation. So does every request until the first result is stored, 150 ms later. Many callers rebuilding the same missing answer at once is a <strong>cache stampede</strong>, also called a thundering herd or a dog-pile.</p>
<p>Its size is the arrival rate times the time to the first fill: 400 a second for 0.15 s is 60 computations of the same list. At 20 ms each, the source’s allowance of 2,000 fills a second lets the fleet run 40 card fills at once. Normal misses already keep about 24 of them busy, and one expiry asks for 60 more, each seven times as long.</p>
<MissFigure kind="stampede" number={1} />
<p>A run against a disposable PostgreSQL and Redis, with the computation modelled as 150 ms, started 61 queries before the first fill was stored at 151 ms. But the extra computations compete with everything else for processors and disks, so the source slows. If each now takes 600 ms, the window stays open four times as long, and 240 computations start, six times the fleet’s fill slots. A stampede on an expensive key feeds itself.</p>
<p>With a 600 ms computation the run started 241 queries. About 0.6 s in, PostgreSQL’s active backends looked like this:</p>
<pre><code>{stampedeActivity}</code></pre>
<p>Hundreds of active copies of one statement, started one after another over the last 0.6 s, are the signature of a stampede. The wait event is <code>PgSleep</code> only because the run modelled the work with <code>pg_sleep</code>; in production it would be CPU, I/O or a lock.</p>
<p>The same estimate tells you when to do nothing. P7’s card, at 250 requests a second and 20 ms a fill, costs about five duplicate reads per expiry, and five runs each made six reads in all. A database absorbs that unnoticed.</p>
<p>A daily hit ratio hides the burst. The list misses 60 of the 12,000 requests in each 30-second TTL, a hit ratio of 99.5%, but those 60 misses arrive together.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="share-work">
<h2 id="share-work">Share the work for one key</h2>
<p>The burst happens because callers cannot see each other’s work. Give each <code>Server</code> a map of loads in progress, keyed by the complete cache key. The first caller to miss registers a load and starts it, and later callers find the entry and wait for its result. When it finishes, every waiter receives its result or its error, and the entry is removed. This is <strong>request coalescing</strong>, also called request collapsing or singleflight, and within one process it turns 60 computations of the list into one.</p>
<p>Three details make it correct. Checking the map and registering a load must be one atomic step, or two callers can both decide they are first. The load checks the cache again before reading the source, in case another process has stored the answer meanwhile. And the map’s key must identify the same answer as the cache key, or one shop’s caller can receive another shop’s card, as <a href={hrefFor('cache-keys')}>Cache keys</a> explains.</p>
<p>A failed load passes its error to every waiter. Nobody caches it as “not found”, because a timeout says nothing about whether P7 exists. Some designs remember the failure itself for a second or so, so that callers fail fast instead of each trying a struggling source. DNS resolvers are required to.</p>
<p>Go’s <code>singleflight</code>, Caffeine’s <code>cache.get(key, loader)</code> and a map of promises elsewhere all share work this way. None decides how long a caller may wait, how long the load may run, or how much source work may start.</p>

<h3 id="bound-wait">Bound the wait as well as the work</h3>
<p>A card request has 150 ms at the <code>Server</code>, and each caller must be able to leave when its time runs out without harming work that others still need. It can leave with an older card only if the cache still holds one that freshness permits. That needs two limits on each entry, a soft TTL and a hard TTL.</p>
<p>The hard limit is the last moment the card may be served. It counts from the card’s read at the source and is set as the Redis expiry. The soft limit, the refresh point, is stored in the value with the read time. Between the two, a lookup returns the card marked as due for replacement, and the caller can join a load with the older card in reserve. After the hard limit the lookup misses outright.</p>
<p>Suppose caller A misses P7’s card at 0 ms and starts a load that will take 100 ms, but A can wait only 50 ms. B joins at 20 ms and C at 40 ms, and each of them can wait 150 ms. In most code the load runs under A’s request context, because A’s handler called it. A’s deadline then cancels the query at 50 ms, and B and C lose an answer that would have arrived at 100 ms.</p>
<p>So the load gets its own lifetime. It is detached from the caller that started it and given its own budget, here 120 ms, twice the card fill’s 60 ms 99th percentile. Each caller waits for the result or for its own deadline, whichever comes first. A caller whose deadline comes first returns an older card it holds, or an error, and starts nothing. In Go, <code>context.WithoutCancel</code> detaches the load, and <code>DoChan</code> lets each caller wait on its own deadline:</p>
<Code code={wrapper} lang="go" theme="css-variables" />
<p>In a run, this wrapper gave B and C the card at 102 ms from one source query. A received an error at 50 ms, or the older card when its lookup had found one. With the load tied to A’s context, all three failed at 50 ms.</p>
<MissFigure kind="sharedLoad" number={2} />
<p>The rule that matters most is in the last branch: a caller that gives up never starts its own read. The proxy cache in nginx lets one request fill a missing entry while others wait. When <code>proxy_cache_lock_timeout</code> runs out, though, a waiting request “will be passed to the proxied server, however, the response will not be cached”. Every waiter becomes uncached origin work at exactly the moment the origin is slow.</p>
<p>When the load’s budget runs out, its waiters receive an error at 120 ms, and the Go client asks PostgreSQL to cancel the query. Asking is not the same as knowing the work has stopped. A cancel request can be lost with a broken connection, and in a run, a client killed one second into an eight-second query left its backend active until the query finished.</p>
<p>Two PostgreSQL settings, both off by default, bound the work where it runs. <code>statement_timeout</code> cancels any statement that runs past its limit, so give the fill role one shorter than the load’s budget. <code>client_connection_check_interval</code> makes a running query notice a vanished client; at 250 ms it removed the killed client’s backend within a second.</p>
<p>Bound the numbers too. <code>singleflight</code> limits neither its keys nor its waiters, so cap both per process, and answer a caller that cannot join as if its deadline had passed.</p>

<h3 id="instances">Across application instances</h3>
<p>The map lives in one process, so each of the 20 <code>Server</code> instances coalesces only its own callers. In the stampede run, 20 groups, each taking every twentieth request, ran 20 list queries for one expiry.</p>
<p>Whether 20 is acceptable depends on the key. Each <code>Server</code> sees about 12 requests a second for P7’s card, so a 20 ms window rarely holds two of them, and five or so duplicate reads per expiry are not worth coordinating. For the list, 20 computations of 150 ms take half the fleet’s 40 fill slots every 30 seconds. The list earns another mechanism, and the card does not.</p>
<p>A <strong>lease</strong> gives one process permission to refill a key for a limited time. In Redis, <code>SET lease:&lt;key&gt; &lt;token&gt; NX PX 200</code> succeeds only if no lease exists. The token is random for this attempt, and the lease vanishes after 200 ms if its holder crashes. The winner computes the list, and a loser serves the older list while its hard limit allows. Facebook’s memcache issued lease tokens at most once every ten seconds per key, and on keys prone to herds its peak database load fell from 17,000 queries a second to 1,300.</p>
<p>A lease saves work; it does not make anything correct. In Martin Kleppmann’s terms it is an efficiency lock, which may occasionally let two holders through at the cost of duplicate work. A correctness lock would need the protected resource itself to refuse an expired holder, and here that job falls to conditional publication.</p>
<p>The weak point is a slow owner. Server A takes a 200 ms lease and starts the list query, which stalls for 900 ms. At 10 ms, Server B finds A’s lease and serves the older list. A’s lease expires at 200 ms while A is still working, so at 210 ms B wins a new lease and computes the list, which it publishes at 360 ms. For those 150 ms the list is computed twice.</p>
<MissFigure kind="lease" number={3} />
<p>At 900 ms A’s query returns, with a list older than B’s. A plain <code>SET</code> would replace B’s list with it, and a plain <code>DEL</code> would remove whatever lease exists by then, perhaps a third <code>Server</code>’s. Both operations must check A’s token atomically, and on Redis 7.0.15 the run printed:</p>
<pre><code>{leaseTrace}</code></pre>
<p>With a plain <code>SET</code>, the run ended with A’s older list in the cache. Redis runs a Lua script without interleaving other commands, so each check is a short script:</p>
<pre><code>{publishCheck}</code></pre>
<p>The release script deletes the lease on the same condition, which Redis 8.4’s <code>DELEX key IFEQ token</code> does in one command. On Redis Cluster, the lease and the list need a shared hash tag such as <code>{'{list:…}'}</code>, so that the script’s two keys sit in one slot.</p>
<p>The lease bounded A’s permission, not A’s work: PostgreSQL never heard of it. With a <code>statement_timeout</code> of 180 ms on A’s fill, PostgreSQL cancelled A’s query at 181 ms with <code>ERROR: canceling statement due to statement timeout</code>, and B computed the list alone. Keep that order, with the statement timeout shorter than the load’s budget and the budget shorter than the lease, and keep the token check, since a process can pause between its query and its publish.</p>
<p>Those values are far too tight for real use. In the slowdown to 600 ms, a 180 ms timeout would cancel every computation, and the list would never refresh. Size all three from the 99th percentile under load, with headroom. Suppose the list’s 99th percentile is about 450 ms. Then a 1 s statement timeout, a 1.2 s budget and a 2 s lease let even a 600 ms computation finish.</p>
<p>If the source is slower still, refreshes fail, and callers receive the older list until its hard limit and a refusal after it. If the lease store is unavailable, fall back to per-process coalescing through the same admission limit. Treating “no lease service” as “everyone may load” would remove the protection just when the cache tier is in trouble.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="early-refresh">
<h2 id="early-refresh">Refresh while an answer is still usable</h2>
<p>So far, callers wait for a replacement. A hot entry can instead be replaced before anyone waits: one request starts a refresh while the others keep receiving the current answer, still inside its hard limit.</p>
<p>The two limits now do separate jobs. The soft limit is when the application starts trying to replace the answer. The hard limit, 30 seconds after the list was read, is the last moment it may be served. A successful refresh stores a new observation with its own 30 seconds. A failed refresh leaves the old answer exactly as old as it was, so refreshing early spends part of the allowance and never extends it.</p>
<MissFigure kind="refresh" number={4} />
<p>HTTP calls this <code>stale-while-revalidate</code>. How long an answer may be served is decided in <a href={hrefFor('freshness')}>Freshness and invalidation</a>; early refresh works inside it.</p>
<p>A fixed refresh point on a hot key brings the stampede back politely. If every request after 25 seconds may refresh, every request before the first refresh lands starts one: 60 again for the list. A fixed point needs coalescing, and for the list a lease.</p>
<p><strong>Probabilistic early expiration</strong>, published as XFetch by Vattani, Chierichetti and Lowenstein, spreads the decision across the requests. Store the computation time Δ with the value, and let each request refresh early if</p>
<p class="miss-formula"><code>now − Δ · β · ln(rand()) ≥ expiry</code></p>
<p>where <code>rand()</code> is uniform between 0 and 1, and β is a tuning factor, 1 by default. Because −ln(rand()) is usually small and occasionally large, refreshes are rare long before expiry and increasingly likely close to it.</p>
<p>The first refresh starts roughly Δ · β · ln(rate · Δ · β) before expiry. For the list that is 0.15 × ln 60, about 0.6 s, and in 2,000 simulated expiries it was 0.7 s on average. Requests that arrive while it runs may start more, 2.8 refreshes per expiry on average and 11 at the 99th percentile. They come from all 20 <code>Server</code> instances, so per-process coalescing hardly helps; if 11 at once is too many, take the lease before refreshing.</p>
<p>XFetch needs no coordination and does no work for keys nobody reads, while a timer refreshing all 50,000 cards every 25 seconds would take the whole allowance. Fixed refresh points with jitter are enough when rate × fill time is small, as for P7’s card at about five. XFetch earns its place where that product is large.</p>

<h3 id="jitter">Spread refreshes across keys</h3>
<p>Jitter solves a different problem: keys written together expire together. If a warm-up fills 10,000 cards in a few seconds, the source sees that warm-up again one TTL later. Choose each key’s refresh point at random inside the allowance, say between 22 and 26 seconds, and never beyond it.</p>
<p>Jitter separates keys, so it does nothing for the 400 callers of one key. And when Redis restarts empty, neither jitter nor early refresh helps, because there is nothing left to refresh. That needs a limit on all the work sent to the source.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="source-budget">
<h2 id="source-budget">Limit source work across all keys</h2>
<p>Coalescing combines callers of one key, but after a cold restart most misses are for different cards. In a simulation of the shop, with 50,000 cards of Zipf-like popularity, per-process coalescing absorbed about one miss in eight, and perfect fleet-wide coalescing a third. Something must cap the rest.</p>
<p>The cap that protects the source is concurrency: how many fills run at once. By Little’s law, fills in progress equal their start rate times their mean duration. At 2,000 fills a second and about 20 ms each, that is 40 fills in flight, or 2 executing slots on each <code>Server</code>. The law needs the mean, and the 60 ms 99th percentile pulls the mean above the median. So 40 slots deliver somewhat fewer than 2,000 fills a second, which errs on the safe side.</p>
<p>A <code>Server</code> whose two slots are busy holds at most one more request in a waiting place, within Google’s SRE advice to keep queues no longer than half the workers. When that request reaches a slot, check its remaining deadline again, and refuse it if the time left no longer covers a fill. A request that finds the slots and the waiting place full is refused at once. Deciding whether to start work at all is <strong>admission control</strong>.</p>
<p>A concurrency limit keeps protecting a source that slows. If fills slow to 40 ms, the 40 slots complete 1,000 a second, and at 200 ms only 200. The slower the source, the less it receives. A rate limit of 2,000 starts a second keeps admitting 2,000 however long each takes, which at 200 ms is 400 fills in flight. Demand fills, refreshes, warm-up reads and retries all count against the same allowance.</p>
<p>Refuse quickly. A rejection that takes a millisecond costs almost nothing, while a queue that holds a request for 140 ms and then times out spends the caller’s whole budget. Every refused caller still needs an answer defined in advance, in this order of preference:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>A copy that freshness still permits</strong>, from Redis or from the copies each <code>Server</code> keeps of cards it served recently. A local copy keeps the hard limit of the read it came from.</li>
<li><strong>A reduced card</strong>: the name and picture, with the price marked as temporarily unavailable. Never GBP 0.00 or “product not found”, which are different answers. Checkout still reads the authoritative price.</li>
<li><strong>A fast refusal</strong>, such as HTTP 503, that callers are told not to retry.</li>
</ul>
<p>Per-<code>Server</code> limits add up, so recheck them whenever the fleet changes. At 30 instances, 2 slots each admit 60 fills in flight, or 3,000 a second, past the 2,500 at which checkout failed. Divide the 40 slots again, which rounds down to 1 per instance, or share one limiter across the fleet, or enforce the limit at the source.</p>

<h3 id="pools">A connection pool is not admission control</h3>
<p>Suppose a teammate proposes a 50-connection pool on each <code>Server</code> and no other limit. Across 20 instances that permits 1,000 connections, ten times PostgreSQL’s default <code>max_connections</code> of 100. The pools themselves never make anyone wait. When fills slow from 20 ms to 200 ms, the normal 1,200 misses a second need 240 fills in flight. That is about 12 per <code>Server</code>, well inside a pool of 50.</p>
<p>The database’s limit fails first. PostgreSQL 16 reserves 3 of its 100 connections for superusers. Once the pools hold about 5 connections per <code>Server</code>, the fill role’s 98th connection is refused with <code>FATAL: remaining connection slots are reserved for roles with the SUPERUSER attribute</code>. Callers needing a new connection get that error at once, with no defined fallback, and retry layers multiply it. The 97 open connections carry more than twice the 40 fills the source can sustain. Raising <code>max_connections</code> to stop the errors lets the database run all 240 at once, six times what it can carry.</p>
<p>Admission limits act before any of this. Two slots and a waiting place per <code>Server</code>, recomputed for 30 instances, refuse the excess in a millisecond with a defined answer. The pool then needs room only for those fills and the <code>Server</code>’s other queries.</p>

<h3 id="batching">Batch distinct misses</h3>
<p>Coalescing cannot combine different keys, but one request can miss many. When the list page finds 8 of its 24 cards missing, one query with <code>WHERE id = ANY($1)</code> fetches all eight, which is the DataLoader pattern. Count the batch against the allowance by its cost.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="cache-unavailable">
<h2 id="cache-unavailable">When the cache is unavailable</h2>
<p>Treating every failed lookup as a miss confuses two events. A miss is an answer: the key is not there. A timeout says nothing about the key and has already spent time, so after a 10 ms Redis timeout a source read gets only the 140 ms that remain. Give cache calls their own timeouts and cap how many may be pending, so that a stalled cache cannot hold every <code>Server</code> waiting.</p>
<p>Then count the work. With a hit ratio h, the source receives the fraction 1 − h of requests. Without the cache it receives them all, 1 / (1 − h) times as many. At 94% that is about 17 times, so 1,200 fills a second become 20,000 attempts. Per-process coalescing joins about 2,300 of them to fills already running, which leaves about 17,700 a second, nearly nine times the allowance.</p>
<p>A better cache makes this worse. Bronson and colleagues, who named metastable failures, use a 90% hit ratio. The application then serves ten times what the database could, but only while the cache is warm. At 99% the factor is 100.</p>
<p>Admission still sends the source its 2,000 fills a second. They answer 10% of requests, and callers who joined them on popular cards add about 2%, so in the simulation about 12% received a full card.</p>
<p>Local copies do much better. The 1,000 most requested cards carry two thirds of all requests. Keeping them within their 30 seconds on 20 instances costs 20 × 1,000 ÷ 30, about 670 fills a second, a third of the allowance. In the simulation those copies answered 66% of requests, and the remaining 1,330 fills a second another 7%. So about 72% received a usable card, and the rest a reduced card or a refusal.</p>
<p>When one of many cache servers fails, Facebook’s memcache sends its keys to a small gutter pool of spare servers, rather than rehashing its hot keys onto the survivors.</p>
<p>After repeated failures, a <code>Server</code> may stop calling the cache and send a few probes to detect its recovery, saving the 10 ms timeout on each request. This is a circuit breaker; AWS warns that its two modes make behaviour hard to test, so test both.</p>

<h3 id="retries">Retries multiply the work</h3>
<p>Suppose the card loader, its database client and the edge proxy each make up to three attempts. In a source brownout every layer sees failures, and each attempt becomes up to three at the next layer down. One request for one card can then cause 3 × 3 × 3 = 27 source queries. AWS’s five-layer version of the example reaches 3⁵ = 243 times the database load.</p>
<p>Retry at one layer: the loader, which knows the remaining deadline and sends every attempt through coalescing and admission. Configure the client and the proxy not to retry these reads, and let the loader make its three attempts only while the deadline allows.</p>
<p>Budget retries across requests too, because a per-request cap still triples the load when everything fails. Google’s SRE book retries only while retries stay under 10% of requests. The AWS SDKs take retries from a 500-token bucket that successes refill. A brownout’s timeouts and server errors are transient errors, which cost 14 tokens a retry, so about 35 failed retries empty it. Mark admission refusals and overload errors as not retryable. Backoff and jitter spread retries out, but they create no capacity.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="cold-recovery">
<h2 id="cold-recovery">Return from a cold cache</h2>
<p>Redis restarts empty at peak. Every request misses, admission holds fills at 2,000 a second, and at first nine requests in ten receive a fallback. Recovery is quick because popularity is skewed. The most requested cards are among the first filled, and each fill turns every later request for that card into a hit. In the simulation, 61% of requests were hits after one second, 70% after two and about 80% after five.</p>
<p>The 1,000 most requested cards serve two thirds of requests and need at least half a second of fills. Under demand fills, 500 of them were back within 0.8 s, 900 within 2.6 s and all of them by about 8 s. The simulation models no expiry, which hides a second wave: cards filled together expire together 30 seconds later, unless their refresh points are jittered.</p>
<MissFigure kind="recovery" number={5} />
<p>Prewarming all 50,000 cards first would be slower. At 2,000 fills a second, the warm-up holds the whole allowance for 25 seconds. In catalogue order it restores 4% of requests each second, so 20% after five seconds, against 80% from demand fills. A targeted warm-up does help: yesterday’s 2,000 most requested cards take one second and restore about 70% of hits. Give a warm-up a share of the allowance, and let demand misses come first.</p>
<p>Bring traffic back the same way, in steps, watching two measurements. Source fill latency should stay near its normal 20 ms median and 60 ms 99th percentile, and admitted source work below 40 fills in flight, with refusals falling. Stop when either fails. In Slack’s February 2022 incident, a first step overloaded the database again, and the team raised the limit “by smaller increments”.</p>

<h3 id="metastable">When the cache cannot refill itself</h3>
<p>Ten minutes after a cache failover, the hit ratio is flat at 31% and database CPU is at 100%. <code>pg_stat_activity</code> lists 90 identical card queries, the oldest running for 4.8 s against an application timeout of 1 s. Almost no fills succeed, and the loader records 2.7 source attempts per request.</p>
<p>This is a <strong>metastable failure</strong>: the trigger, the cold cache, has passed, but a loop keeps the system failed. The low hit ratio overloads the database, so queries take longer than 1 s, and the application gives up before any fill stores its answer.</p>
<p>The abandoned queries keep running, and each is replaced by a retry, so the database does nearly three times the work of the requests it is failing. Load falling back to normal would not cure it, and a study of real metastable failures found retries sustaining more than half of them. Two changes break the loop:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Stop the amplification.</strong> Turn off retries outside the loader, budget them inside it, and put the admission limit in front of the database. Admitted fills then finish in their normal 20 ms and store their answers, and the hit ratio starts to rise.</li>
<li><strong>Stop abandoned work at the source.</strong> Set the fill role’s <code>statement_timeout</code> no longer than the application’s budget, so that queries nobody is waiting for are cancelled.</li>
</ol>
<p>Then raise traffic in steps. Without an admission limit, the emergency equivalent is shedding load at the edge. In Facebook’s 2010 outage, a configuration check treated database errors as invalid values and deleted cached keys, and the site was turned off to break the loop.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="exercise">
<h2 id="exercise">Exercise the whole design</h2>
<p>Each mechanism has a failure it exists for, so cause each failure on purpose, with the cache disabled as well as empty:</p>
<div class="cache-table-scroll"><table>
<caption>What each rehearsal should show</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Change</th><th scope="col">Expected observation</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">Slow the list’s computation</th><td>One load per key per <code>Server</code>, or one per fleet with the lease. Callers leave at their deadlines without starting reads; the statement timeout ends the query.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Fail an early refresh</th><td>The older answer is served until its hard limit, and not after.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Miss on many distinct keys</th><td>Fills in flight stay within the fleet limit; refused callers receive the defined fallback within milliseconds.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Make Redis unreachable</th><td>Source work stays inside the allowance; the lease falls back to per-process coalescing.</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Restart Redis empty</th><td>Hits return in popularity order; warm-up and retries spend the same allowance.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Count useful replies, fallbacks, refusals and source work separately. Otherwise fast refusals flatter the latency while fewer customers receive cards.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="numbers">
<h2 id="numbers">Where the numbers come from</h2>
<p>The shop’s production scale is invented at realistic magnitudes and describes no running service. The runs used a disposable PostgreSQL 16.15 and Redis 7.0.15 with Go 1.24.7 on 6 November 2026. Their queries used <code>pg_sleep</code> to model the work, so durations were chosen, while counts, states, errors and replies were observed. The XFetch figures come from 2,000 simulated expiries, and the outage and recovery shares from a simulation of the example. They show orders of magnitude, not predictions.</p>
</section>

